-- Retroactive cleanup for the new isLikelyNonCorporateRole() denylist
-- (_shared/pipeline/relevance.ts, see JOB_ENGINE_ARCHITECTURE.md's dated
-- entry for this change). UC Portal serves UCLA business-club students
-- seeking white-collar corporate roles (finance/consulting/tech/IB/general
-- corporate-office work) -- not manual-trade/hourly-operations work and not
-- direct clinical/patient-care work, regardless of how well-classified or
-- entry-level those postings are.
--
-- 1,207 of the 3,284 currently-active postings match the new denylist:
--   - 1,126 of Carvana''s 1,474 active postings (manual/hourly automotive
--     operations -- detailers, technicians, CDL drivers, security guards,
--     painters, mechanics, etc.). 348 Carvana postings survive, including
--     99 of the 101 that already carry a real O*NET job_function_id
--     classification (Operations Manager, Strategy Analyst, Product
--     Manager - Inventory, Team Lead/Market Operations Manager, Coordinator
--     - HR Operations, etc.). The other 2 classified postings that do get
--     removed are both the same title, ''Technician I/II, Facilities,
--     Property Operations'' -- a building-facilities maintenance role, not
--     a business/finance/consulting/tech role, despite carrying an O*NET
--     classification (O*NET classifies all occupations, not just
--     UC-relevant ones -- classification was never a relevance proxy, see
--     this doc''s prior Carvana findings).
--   - 81 of Charlie Health''s 251 active postings (direct clinical/
--     patient-care work -- Licensed Mental Health Therapist, Crisis
--     Intervention Specialist, Clinical Admissions Therapist, SUD/Mental
--     Health Group Facilitators, Care Coach, Care Navigator, etc.). 170
--     Charlie Health postings survive, including all 7 that already carry
--     an O*NET classification (Commercial Strategy Associate/Manager,
--     Growth Strategy Analyst x2, Strategy & Operations Analyst x2, Care
--     Strategy & Operations Analyst/Associate) and all ~130 Territory
--     Manager field-sales postings (a real business-development role, not
--     clinical, even the ones with "SUD" in the title -- see
--     relevance.ts''s header comment for why bare "sud" was deliberately
--     left out of the pattern). This supersedes Stage 4''s earlier
--     "Charlie Health is a deliberate keep-both-halves case" decision --
--     the user has since scoped UC Portal to white-collar corporate roles
--     only, so the clinical half no longer qualifies either.
--   - 0 postings removed from any of the other 10 active companies
--     (Stripe, Databricks, Brex, IMC, Figma, Airbnb, Coinbase, Robinhood,
--     Deloitte, Guild) -- the pattern was validated against every active
--     company''s real titles specifically to avoid false positives (e.g.
--     bare "security"/"delivery"/"tech"/"operator" would have wrongly
--     caught real Security Engineer, Delivery Solutions Architect, Tech
--     Lead, and Operator Tooling titles at these companies).
--
-- Job IDs are listed explicitly (computed client-side with the exact same
-- regex as the deployed isLikelyNonCorporateRole(), not re-derived in SQL)
-- rather than reimplementing the regex in Postgres'' own dialect, following
-- the same precedent as 20260824120000_remove_senior_role_postings.sql
-- (word-boundary syntax differs between the two: \y vs \b).
--
-- job_sources and duplicate_candidates both have ON DELETE CASCADE FKs to
-- jobs.id (confirmed via information_schema before writing this migration),
-- so a plain delete from jobs would cascade correctly on its own, but this
-- explicitly deletes the dependents first anyway for auditability, matching
-- the prior cleanup migration''s pattern -- extended here to also cover
-- duplicate_candidates.job_id_b, which that migration didn''t. Verified
-- separately that opportunity_submissions, saved_jobs, and
-- tracked_applications are all empty in the live table (no NO ACTION FK
-- violation risk, nothing to clean up there).

